Subject: identifiability of random site effects in static occupancy model
Message-ID: <gTRU7QvlujY.fdk3IjYIBQAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/gTRU7QvlujY/m/fdk3IjYIBQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear all,

with some help by Ken I completed the code for running a simulation-based 'test' of parameter identifiability for random site effects in the simplest static occupancy model. Compared to the earlier file I had sent today, I increased both the number of visits (from 3 to 6) and the values of the intercepts of occupancy and detection --- when checking for parameter identifiability (which denotes our ability in principle to estimate a parameter for a certain type of data set), we need a best-case scenario. That is, we want to make the information content high.

So, for 100 simulated data sets, here is a visual summary of the first case where we want to estimate random site effects in the occupancy part of the model ---- this does not look good !

The intercepts for occupancy and detection are not bad at all, but the estimate of the standard deviation of the random site effects in occupancy bear absolutely no relationship with the known true values that we use when simulating a data set.

And here are the results for 100 simulated data sets for the other case, where we have random site effects in the detection part of the model --- this does look good.

There is some sort of discontinuity in the plot on the left, which I'd probably investigate a little further if I were writing a paper about this, but for now, I claim that this is the simulation-based 'proof' that this model is in fact identifiable.

Notes:
I put 'proof' in quotes, because this is of course not a real mathematical proof of identifiability. But for us ecologists, this is about as good a 'proof' as we can ever hope to get ourselves.
When the data contain less information (e.g., when we have fewer reps per site or smaller values of occupancy and especially of detection), things may break down and we may no longer be able to actually estimate the parameters. So whenever you're in doubt, running a little simulation may be well worth the effort.
This shows once again the power of 'experimental statistics' to help us understand complicated things in statistical modeling.

Best regards  --- Marc

