MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you put the covariates on each species abundance in the first model (hierarchical distance sampling model) and have covariate effects be drawn from community-level hyper-parameters (just like the intercept), you can then derive covariate relationships with diversity from that model without the need for running a second model. The first model would in effect already describe covariate relationships with diversity as an emergent property of species level covariate relationships, so you would then just need to derive the relationships with diversity by calculating predicted diversity at different values of covariates included in the model. This is the usual way of doing things with these hierarchical community models (as far as I am aware). A major advantage of hierarchical models is integration to avoid the need for staged analyses. It looks to me that Sollmann et al. did indeed include covariates on species level abundance models within their community model. This is the approach I think you'd want unless I'm missing something unique to your study or data.

